tiny386: running on the Linux frame buffer console of a small RISC-V board

Against https://github.com/hchunhui/tiny386, branch master, commit
9ed786b4dd9698306dccdb7595858dee10949994 (2026-10-08).
Apply with: git apply tiny386-korvo.patch

Written for the ESP32-S31-Korvo-1 running esp32-s31-linux: one RV32IMAC
processor at 320 MHz, 16 MB of memory in all, every store to which goes
straight out to the PSRAM while the LCD is on, an 800x480 RGB565 frame
buffer with one mode, and a keyboard and a shell on tty1 as the only way
in.  Nothing in it is specific to that board.  Nothing changes unless it is
asked for when building: a build of the other front ends with their own
flags is as it was, but for the VGA's fixes below, which are for every
build.  The changes are offered under the licence of tiny386 (BSD 3-clause,
see LICENSE).

unaligned.h (new), i386.c, pc.c, vga.c
    With -DI386_ALIGNED_ONLY the emulator makes no load or store of 16 or 32
    bits at an address that is not aligned: one that is not is made of
    smaller ones that are.  As released, guest memory is read and written
    through pointers that are as aligned as the guest's addresses were (the
    comment in i386.c says so: "only works on hosts that are little-endian
    and support unaligned access").  Where the host's processor does not do
    such an access itself, each one is a trap into the firmware, which does
    it by hand: it works, very much slower, and nothing says so.  About one
    guest instruction in eight makes one while FreeDOS starts.

vga.c, vga.h
    With -DBPP=16 -DVGA_STORE_CHANGED a pixel is stored only where the
    screen does not show it already, in one store of 16 bits (there were
    two of 8 bits for each pixel of a graphics mode, changed or not).
    In that build the 16-colour modes (640x480, which Windows' standard VGA
    driver uses, and 320x200, mode 0Dh, where each pixel is two dots wide)
    are also drawn another way: eight pixels at a time, from the one word
    of video memory that holds their four planes, and not at all where
    that word is what it was at the last picture.  As released every pixel
    of the screen is worked out at every picture, about 39 instructions
    each.
    As released, a graphics screen is also cleared at every write to a
    register of the CRT controller, and the BIOS writes two of them for
    every character it prints.  Here the clear waits for the next picture
    and is left out if that picture is where the last one was with black
    around it, since the screen then comes out the same.  Every clear makes
    the quick way forget what it knew of the screen.
    A change to a text mode clears the screen, and so does text of another
    size or place (see the fixes below).  When the two come together the
    second clear is left out: nothing has been drawn since the first.
    vga_planar_fast = 0 turns all of this off.  tests/vgatest.c compares
    the two ways pixel for pixel, picture after picture.

vga.c, vga.h, pc.c: what the VGA does that tiny386 did not
    Found by tests/bios-tests.sh, which holds every int 10h function and
    the register tricks DOS programs use against what a VGA does.  None
    is behind a build flag:
    * the line compare (split screen), with the panning dropped below it
      if AR10h bit 5 says so;
    * horizontal pel panning (AR13h) in the graphics modes;
    * mode 0Dh, 320x200 in 16 colours, at its full width: it showed the
      left half of its picture;
    * text, in builds without SCALE_* and SWAPXY (whose text is laid out
      as it was): rows from the top of the picture and cut where they
      fall, so 28 rows sit where a VGA has them; the preset row scan
      (CR08h, smooth scrolling); 40 columns two pixels to a dot; the
      screen cleared when the text moves or changes size, so nothing of
      the last one is left around it;
    * text, in every build: blinking characters and the eight backgrounds
      that go with them (AR10h bit 3); the underline (CR14h); a cursor
      taller than 8 lines;
    * the PEL mask (3C6h);
    * video memory shut off to the CPU when misc output bit 1 is clear;
    * VBE clears video memory when it is turned on, as QEMU does, and not
      at every write to its enable register (4F08h cleared the picture);
    * the monochrome CRT controller and status ports (3B4h, 3B5h, 3BAh)
      reach the VGA, which a BIOS needs for mode 07h (SeaVGABIOS, as
      tiny386 ships it, writes them too early and gets a black screen);
    * the picture's address wraps at 256 KB in the VGA's own modes, as on
      a VGA, and pc.c puts VGA_MEM_SLACK (16 KB) of zeroes behind video
      memory for a line that runs past its end: a picture that started
      near the end was read on past it, as far as the rest of the
      picture went; in a VBE mode a line that starts past the end is not
      drawn, and the line compare is not used, as on the Bochs VBE card;
    * the cursor's glyph has all its lines (it was read past its end).

pc.c
    PC_STEP_COUNT, the number of guest instructions between two looks at
    the clock, the keyboard and the screen, can be given when building.
    What the BIOS writes to its debug port goes to PC_DEBUGCON (stdout if
    nothing is said, as before).

ide.c, ide.h, misc.c, misc.h
    ide_write_count() and ide_flush(), emulink_write_count() and
    emulink_flush(): how many writes the guest has made to its disk and
    floppy images, and calls that return when they are on the medium.
    Every write is handed to the kernel when it is made (fflush), so that
    an emulator that is killed leaves nothing behind in a buffer of its
    own.  emulink_floppy_size_ok() says whether a file is the size of a
    floppy that the emulator knows; it stops on one that is not.
    The stores of 16 and 32 bits into the buffer of an ATAPI answer, which
    are at odd addresses, are made a byte at a time.

term.c, term.h
    Ctrl+] can be given to the front end (term_quit_key) and then goes no
    further: in -term the terminal is raw and has no key that ends a program.

korvo/main.c (new)
    A front end for such a board, made from sdl/main.c and main.c: the
    picture straight into the frame buffer through SDL 1.2's fbcon driver,
    or the text screen on the terminal (-term), or nothing (-none); drawing
    held to a share of the time; a look at how much memory the machine has
    before any is taken; Ctrl+Alt+Backspace to quit; the guest's writes put
    on the card when it has been quiet for two seconds; messages kept off
    the screen; a count of instructions and a rate at the end, printed on
    every way out when the screen is text again.  It refuses a serial port
    (tiny386's is the terminal's keyboard) and a floppy image of a size it
    does not know.  It expects
    SDL 1.2.15 with the frame buffer patch of the Wolfenstein 3D port for
    the same board (SDL-1.2.15-korvo-fbcon.patch).

korvo/guard.c, korvo/guard.h (new)
    The console guard: a second small process that puts the console back
    in text mode if the emulator is killed outright, which is what the
    kernel does to a process when memory runs out.  --fix-console does the
    same by hand.  Both also take a terminal out of the state that -term
    puts it in.

korvo/hostbench.c, korvo/hostbench.h (new)
    --hostbench: what loads and stores, aligned and not, cost on the
    machine it is run on.

